\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\textbf{How the Texas Commissioner of Insurance Takes Office}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Elected Position} – The Commissioner of Insurance is a statewide \textbf{elected} official in Texas.  The office is not appointed by the governor or the legislature.
  \item \textbf{Election Cycle} – The commissioner serves a \textbf{four‑year term} and may be re‑elected.  Elections are held in \textbf{general elections} (held in even‑numbered years).  The seat is on the ballot every four years; there is no staggered mid‑term election.
  \item \textbf{Partisan Ballot} – The office is \textbf{partisan}.  Candidates run under a party label (most commonly Republican or Democratic) and must file with the Texas Secretary of State.
  \item \textbf{Qualifications} – To appear on the ballot a candidate must:
    \begin{itemize}
      \item Be a Texas resident for at least two years preceding the election.
      \item Be at least 30 years old.
      \item Be a qualified voter in Texas.
      \item Meet any additional filing requirements (e.g., filing fee, petition signatures).
    \end{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Primary Elections} – Political parties hold \textbf{primaries} (or conventions) to select their nominee.  Voters in each party choose one candidate per party who will appear on the general election ballot.
  \item \textbf{General Election} – All candidates on the ballot (including third‑party or independent candidates) are voted on by the electorate statewide.  The candidate who receives a plurality of votes wins the office.
  \item \textbf{Vote Certification} – After the election, the Texas Secretary of State certifies the results.  The winner is then declared the Commissioner of Insurance.
  \item \textbf{Inauguration} – The elected commissioner takes office on \textbf{January 1} following the election (or as otherwise specified by state law).  The oath of office is administered by the governor or a designated official.
  \item \textbf{Vacancies} – If the office becomes vacant (e.g., resignation, death, removal), the \textbf{governor} may appoint an interim commissioner until a special election is held, per Texas Election Code.
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Key Takeaway:} In Texas, the Commissioner of Insurance is placed in office through a \textbf{statewide partisan election} held every four years, with the winner inaugurated on January 1 of the following year.  The governor can only appoint a commissioner to fill a vacancy; otherwise, the position is filled solely by the electorate.
\end{minipage}
}<|im_end|>

