\text{The “State of the Nation” address is an annual speech given by a country’s head of state.  Its timing varies by nation:} \\
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{United States (State of the Union):} Delivered before a joint session of Congress, usually in January or February.  In recent years it has been broadcast at about 9:00 p.m. Eastern Time.  
  \item \textbf{Philippines (State of the Nation Address):} Given at the opening of the regular session of Congress, traditionally on the last Monday of July, typically around 9:00 a.m. Manila time.  
  \item \textbf{Other countries:} Many parliamentary systems schedule the address at the start of the legislative year (e.g., Canada’s “Throne Speech” in early spring, Australia’s opening of Parliament in May–June).  
\end{itemize}
\text{Thus, the exact time depends on the country and the year, but it is always tied to the opening of the parliamentary or legislative calendar.}
